<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    PATH 1, direct mapping, on the DEFAULT entity manager.

    The class is the entity and the column list is the whole mapping. The only non-built-in type is
    `Id`, registered once for the whole project in config/packages/doctrine.php, so the per-entity
    mapping cost here is zero. That is what ADR 003 means by path 1 and it is why a credential never
    needed a reader, a writer, a tracker or a projection.

    The mapping is XML rather than attributes for the reason every other mapping in this tree is:
    the demonstration forbids `Doctrine\ORM\Mapping` inside the namespaces it describes. `Ui` is
    allowed to name Doctrine, so the rule does not bind here, but a single mapping written the other
    way would read as an exception somebody was making rather than a convention being kept.

    It is on the default manager rather than on `read_model` because nothing about it is a
    projection: it is written once and read by identity. The `read_model` manager exists so a query
    handler cannot reach a write-model repository by accident, and a credential is on neither side of
    that fence.

    TWO UNIQUE CONSTRAINTS, AND THEY ARE THE TWO WAYS IN.

    `username` is unique because ADR 009 rule 10 makes it what a human types into the sign-in form,
    so `CredentialProvider::loadUserByIdentifier()` has to return one row or none. `member_id` is
    unique because a member has one credential, which is what makes resolving a principal from a
    token's `sub` claim an identity lookup as well. Both are lookups by a unique key rather than
    filters, which is what keeps this table on path 1 (ADR 004 rule 1: a repository answers by
    identity, a read model answers by criteria).

    `id` carries no index of its own: it is the primary key, and it is now an ordinary surrogate
    again. It used to be the opaque bearer token, which ADR 009 rule 9 corrects; the token is a JWT
    and what a revocation holds is its `jti`, in `ui_revoked_token` beside this one.

    NO `session_id`, and no session table anywhere. ADR 009: a session is PHP's, Symfony expires it
    and garbage-collects it, and a row beside it is a second lifetime to keep in step with the first.

    No `updated_at`, no `last_used_at`, no `failed_attempts`, no `email_verified_at`. Every one of
    those is an invariant waiting to be written, and ADR 008 says that the day credentials grow
    invariants they stop being a `Ui` table and become an `Identity` context.

    `username` has no length below 255 and no format check, because what a username may contain is
    policy nobody has decided; see `Credential`'s docblock. The column enforces the one thing that is
    true whatever the answer.
-->
<doctrine-mapping xmlns="http://doctrine-project.org/schemas/orm/doctrine-mapping"
                  xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
                  xsi:schemaLocation="http://doctrine-project.org/schemas/orm/doctrine-mapping https://www.doctrine-project.org/schemas/orm/doctrine-mapping.xsd">
    <entity name="App\Shared\Infrastructure\Security\Credential" table="ui_credential">
        <unique-constraints>
            <unique-constraint name="uniq_credential_username" columns="username"/>
            <unique-constraint name="uniq_credential_member" columns="member_id"/>
        </unique-constraints>

        <id name="id" type="Id" column="id">
            <generator strategy="NONE"/>
        </id>

        <field name="memberId" type="Id" column="member_id" nullable="false"/>
        <field name="username" type="string" column="username" length="255" nullable="false"/>
        <field name="passwordHash" type="string" column="password_hash" length="255" nullable="false"/>
        <field name="roles" type="json" column="roles" nullable="false"/>
        <field name="createdAt" type="datetime_immutable" column="created_at" nullable="false"/>
    </entity>
</doctrine-mapping>
